// panic
// #4375, the base case of the non-negativity induction. `proveInductionBounds`
// drops the element load's unsigned test only when the header param is proven
// `>= 0`, and the only thing between a wrong proof and a read BEHIND the buffer
// is `bceIsNonNegConst` refusing a negative literal.
//
// `i` starts at a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT -1 on purpose: a runtime-computed
// negative start is refused for a second, unrelated reason (it is not a
// constant at all), so it could not see whether the sign test itself works.
//
// THE READ IS THE FIRST THING IN THE BODY, and that placement is what makes
// this fixture able to fail. `slice_len` is a memory read, so any call between
// the header and the access ends the availability chain and the two lengths
// stay distinct value ids — the pass then declines for a reason that has
// nothing to do with the sign, and the fixture proves nothing. Written with
// the access inside `eprint`'s argument, this case SURVIVES a mutant that
// accepts a negative literal.
//
// The failure it catches is silent: an unsigned `-1` addresses the word behind
// the element buffer, which is live heap. Measured against that mutant here,
// the loop runs one extra iteration and prints `i=-1 read=0` with exit 0.
// #4346 measured the same shape on the string byte load — four of five
// fixtures exited 0 with a plausible value. Stderr is the stream a `// panic`
// case compares.
import { args } from "std/os"

fn main() {
  let xs = [11, 22, 33]
  xs = append(xs, 44 - len(args()))
  let i = -1
  let got = 0
  while (i < len(xs)) {
    got = xs[i]
    eprint("i=${i} read=${got}\n")
    i = i + 1
  }
  eprint("unreachable\n")
}
